("""What happened next is the substance of this paper. Fully nested cross-validation — repeating the whole procedure inside every fold — cut both models to about 0.58 and made them indistinguishable from each other. That is not a failure of the compression argument; it is a demonstration that the *apparent* superiority of both models over chance was largely selection optimism, and that a study reporting only apparent C-indices in this design is reporting an artefact. Repetition of the bootstrap under ten independent seeds then showed that MAFG's stability is a threshold artefact: its π ranges from 0.690 to 0.805 across seeds and falls below the prespecified 0.75 in two of them, at which point the "two-gene signature" is a one-gene signature. KIF15, by contrast, never fell below 0.875. We had reported a two-gene score; the data support one robustly stable gene and one threshold-marginal gene.""",
 """What happened next is the substance of this paper. Fully nested cross-validation cut both models to about 0.58 and made them indistinguishable — not a failure of the compression argument, but a demonstration that the *apparent* superiority of both models over chance was largely selection optimism, and that a study reporting only apparent C-indices in this design is reporting an artefact. Repetition of the bootstrap under ten seeds then showed MAFG's stability to be a threshold artefact: its π ranges from 0.690 to 0.805 and falls below the prespecified 0.75 in two seeds, at which point the "two-gene signature" is a one-gene signature, whereas KIF15 never fell below 0.875. We had reported a two-gene score; the data support one robustly stable gene and one threshold-marginal gene."""),

("""The external test settled it. In GSE14520 — 242 tumours, 96 deaths, median follow-up 57 months, the standard resource for this purpose — the frozen MRG-2 score gave a C-index of 0.551, a hazard ratio of 1.14 that was not significant, an adjusted hazard ratio of 1.05, and a position at the 47th percentile of 3,000 random two-gene panels. In the same cohort a generic 20-gene proliferation score reached 0.596 and a log-rank *P* of 7.2×10⁻⁴. The ordering that held in TCGA — MRG-2 above proliferation — inverted in validation. That inversion, together with the monotone decline from 0.677 to 0.576 to 0.551, is what selection optimism looks like when it is measured instead of assumed.""",
 """The external test settled it. In GSE14520 — 242 tumours, 96 deaths, the standard resource for this purpose — the frozen MRG-2 score gave a C-index of 0.551, a non-significant hazard ratio of 1.14, an adjusted hazard ratio of 1.05, and a position at the 47th percentile of 3,000 random two-gene panels, while a generic 20-gene proliferation score in the same cohort reached 0.596 and a log-rank *P* of 7.2×10⁻⁴. The ordering that held in TCGA — MRG-2 above proliferation — inverted in validation. That inversion, with the monotone decline from 0.677 to 0.576 to 0.551, is what selection optimism looks like when it is measured instead of assumed."""),

("""What is specific to HCC, and what was not expected from the glioma analysis of the same panel, remains worth recording even though it did not translate externally. Three of the five genes that survive multiple-testing correction are redox genes — MAFG, TXNRD1 and OSGIN2 — and MAFG, the marginal member of the score, is a small-Maf protein that heterodimerises with NRF2 and drives antioxidant-response-element transcription [33,34]. In a tumour whose therapeutic backbone kills through mitochondrial reactive oxygen species and whose resistance is redox-mediated [9,10], a prognostic signal led by the redox arm is biologically coherent. But coherence is not transport, and the redox reading rests on a gene whose own selection stability is threshold-dependent. The panel supplied the selection signal; the pathway's machinery partially survived into the score; that is as far as the data take us, and it is not far enough to call the score a readout of mitoxyperilysis.""",
 """What is specific to HCC, and was not expected from the glioma analysis of the same panel, remains worth recording even though it did not translate externally. Three of the five genes surviving multiple-testing correction are redox genes — MAFG, TXNRD1 and OSGIN2 — and MAFG, the marginal member of the score, is a small-Maf protein that heterodimerises with NRF2 to drive antioxidant-response-element transcription [33,34]. In a tumour whose therapeutic backbone kills through mitochondrial reactive oxygen species and whose resistance is redox-mediated [9,10], a prognostic signal led by the redox arm is biologically coherent. But coherence is not transport, and the redox reading rests on a gene whose own selection stability is threshold-dependent. The panel supplied the selection signal and the pathway's machinery partially survived into the score; that is as far as the data take us."""),

("""Seven limitations bound the interpretation. First, and most importantly, the compressed score failed external validation; every statement in Sections 3.3–3.5 is a statement about the discovery cohort and none of them should be generalised. Second, the discovery cohort is modest (129 deaths), so the apparent C-index of 0.677 retained about 0.10 units of optimism, as the nested analysis quantifies; the external value is the one to carry forward. Third, the two genes are not equally supported by the data: KIF15 is stable under every seed, MAFG is not. Fourth, the discovery and validation systems are mismatched — the panel came from mouse macrophages under acute starvation, the target tissue is human bulk tumour, and neither retained gene is a pathway executioner — and the external platform (Affymetrix HG-U133A) is older and less sensitive than RNA sequencing, which may depress measured discrimination for reasons unrelated to biology; we cannot separate those two explanations. Fifth, no within-cohort tumour-versus-normal comparison is possible in the PanCancer Atlas LIHC cohort, and the reference-normal profile used in its place draws on a different normal-tissue resource, so Section 3.9 is descriptive only. Sixth, the immune analysis is a marker-mean proxy, not corrected for tumour purity and not a deconvolution. Seventh, no prospective or decision-impact analysis was performed and no clinical utility is claimed for any score in this paper — including for a drug-sensitivity analysis (GDSC/CTRP), which we omit because cell-line pharmacogenomic resources are not patient-matched to these cohorts and predicted IC₅₀ values from a fitted model are extrapolation rather than observation.""",
 """Seven limitations bound the interpretation. First, and most importantly, the compressed score failed external validation; every statement in Sections 3.3–3.5 concerns the discovery cohort and none should be generalised. Second, the discovery cohort is modest (129 deaths), so the apparent C-index of 0.677 retained about 0.10 units of optimism; the external value is the one to carry forward. Third, the two genes are not equally supported: KIF15 is stable under every seed, MAFG is not. Fourth, the discovery and validation systems are mismatched — the panel came from mouse macrophages under acute starvation, the target tissue is human bulk tumour, neither retained gene is a pathway executioner, and the external platform (Affymetrix HG-U133A) is older and less sensitive than RNA sequencing; we cannot separate those explanations. Fifth, no within-cohort tumour-versus-normal comparison is possible, so Section 3.9 is descriptive only. Sixth, the immune analysis is a marker-mean proxy, not corrected for tumour purity and not a deconvolution. Seventh, no prospective or decision-impact analysis was performed and no clinical utility is claimed for any score in this paper — including for a drug-sensitivity analysis (GDSC/CTRP), which we omit because cell-line pharmacogenomic resources are not patient-matched to these cohorts and predicted IC₅₀ values from a fitted model are extrapolation rather than observation."""),
